,[253365,"reichtet aus","verb","second-person plural subjunctive II",2]]},"revanchist":{"entries":[["revanchist",null,null,"Possibly borrowed from Russian реванши́ст (revanšíst), from French revanchiste. By surface analysis, Revanche + -ist.",{"g":"m","decl":"|en|en|en|en|en|en|en"}]],"senses":[[36121,"revanchist","noun","revanchist",0,"m"]]},"s":{"entries":[["s",null,"/ɛs/","For the origin of the letter, see s.\nOrthography and pronunciation\nSingle s in prevocalic position is pronounced /z/, except when it follows an obstruent within the word stem (e.g. Achse, bugsieren, Lotse, schubsen). /s/ is usually retained in recent borrowings from English (e.g. Sex), to a lesser degree also in recent borrowings from other languages (e.g. Salsa). Words from the classical languages and pre-1900 loanwords behave like native words.\nStem-initial st and sp are pronounced /ʃt/ and /ʃp/ in native words. The same is generally true for classical and pre-1900 loanwords, though in this case we find some exceptions which are at least optionally pronounced with /st/, /sp/ (e.g. Stil, Stola). Recent borrowings tend to retain the original sounds. Again, this tendency is stronger with English than with other source languages (cf. e.g. Spaghetti with /ʃp/).\nThe trigraph sch is pronounced /ʃ/, except when there is an intervening morpheme boundary, which normally happens only with the diminutive suffix -chen (cf. e.g. Häuschen).\nOtherwise, pre-consonantal and word-final s is always pronounced /s/. This also includes st, sp when they are not stem-initial. The same is generally true for doubled ss. There are, however, a few words in which ss may – optionally – be pronounced /z/ (e.g. Fussel, Massel, quasseln, Schussel). For the orthographic choice between ss and ß, see the latter.",{"w":"s","g":"n","decl":"|||||||","alt":[["n","|||||||"],["n","|||||||","S"]]}]],"senses":[[9205,"s","character","The nineteenth letter of the German alphabet, called es and written in the Latin script.",0],[9206,"s","noun","abbreviation of Süd (“south”)",1],[9207,"s","noun","abbreviation of Springer (“knight”)",2]]},"selbstgefälligen":{"entries":[["selbstgefälligen",null,null,null]],"senses":[[311540,"selbstgefälligen","adj","inflection of selbstgefällig:",0],[311541,"selbstgefälligen","adj","strong genitive masculine/neuter singular",1],[311542,"selbstgefälligen","adj","weak/mixed genitive/dative all-gender singular",2],[311543,"selbstgefälligen","adj","strong/weak/mixed accusative masculine singular",3],[311544,"selbstgefälligen","adj","strong dative plural",4],[311545,"selbstgefälligen","adj","weak/mixed all-case plural",5]],"inflections":[["selbstgefälligen","selbstgefällig","genitive,masculine,singular,strong,without-article"]]},"slowenischere":{"entries":[["slowenischere",null,null,null]],"senses":[[485016,"slowenischere","adj","inflection of slowenisch:",0],[485017,"slowenischere","adj","strong/mixed nominative/accusative feminine singular comparative degree",1],[485018,"slowenischere","adj","strong nominative/accusative plural comparative degree",2],[485019,"slowenischere","adj","weak nominative all-gender singular comparative degree",3],[485020,"slowenischere","adj","weak accusative feminine/neuter singular comparative degree",4]],"inflections":[["slowenischere","slowenisch","comparative,feminine,nominative,singular,strong,without-article"]]},"spitze":{"entries":[["spitze",null,"/ˈʃpɪt͡sə/","From Middle High German spitze, from Old High German spizza, from Proto-Germanic *spitjaz, related to *spitō (“spike”). Compare English spit (“skewer”), Swedish spets (“point, tip; lace”).",{"g":"f","decl":"||||n|n|n|n"}]],"senses":[[28671,"spitze","noun","cusp",0,"f"],[28672,"spitze","noun","point, tip, peak",1,"f"],[28673,"spitze","noun","top, front, lead",2,"f"],[28674,"spitze","noun","spike",3,"f"],[28675,"spitze","noun","lace (textile pattern)",4,"f"],[28676,"spitze","noun","striker",5,"f"],[28677,"spitze","noun","leadership",6,"f",["figuratively"]],[28678,"spitze","noun","